  "id": "COMPOUND:3069 - PROTEIN:1234",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential mechanism by which phenol may interact with Potassium channel subfamily K member 3, through its interaction with Sodium channel subunit beta-2. This pathway is supported by three distinct but overlapping routes, all of which indicate that phenol acts as a target for compounds that ultimately target the potassium channel subfamily K member 3. The shared involvement of Sodium channel subunit beta-2 as a target of multiple compounds (e.g., (Z)-N-[(4-hydroxy-3-methoxyphenyl)methyl]-9-methyldec-7-enamide, Longispinogenin, and Amidepsine D) suggests a common biological pathway, possibly involving modulation of ion channel activity. The presence of a final_score of 0.465 across all three paths indicates a moderate level of confidence in the observed relationships, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for phenol to influence potassium channel function through its interaction with sodium channels, which in turn may affect cellular electrophysiology and contribute to therapeutic effects.",
